        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90004</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Frailty may influence treatment tolerance but is not routinely measured in metastatic breast cancer. We examined whether a brief electronic frailty assessment predicted unplanned hospitalization. <bold>Methods: </bold>Patients beginning a new systemic regimen completed a 12-item frailty questionnaire. Hospitalizations within 90 days were identified from the electronic record. Multivariable logistic regression adjusted for age, performance status, and treatment class. <bold>Results: </bold>Of 203 patients, 46 were classified as frail. Unplanned hospitalization occurred in 30.4% of frail patients and 11.5% of nonfrail patients. Frailty remained independently associated with hospitalization. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>A brief frailty assessment identified patients at increased risk of early hospitalization. Please let me know if you would like me to make this more concise or add references. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
